>
  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">Who can get a Texas learner license?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      People 15 or older but under 18. §521.222 authorizes a learner license for a person who &ldquo;is 15 years of age or older but
      under 18 years of age&rdquo;, who has passed the classroom phase of an approved driver education course and every examination other
      than the driving test. DPS&rsquo;s own learner license page lists the same age range. It is the first half of the graduated
      licensing system Texas built for teenagers.
    </p>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      As an adult you are outside that system. You apply for the driver license, take the tests, and are issued the license. There is
      no intermediate permit stage designed for you and no six-month holding period to serve.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">How does an adult get driving practice without a permit?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Through a driving school, or supervised practice where your circumstances allow it. The learner license exists so a teenager
      can legally accumulate driving hours before the test. An adult who has never driven needs the same hours and has no
      equivalent instrument. A commercial driving school supplies an instructor and a dual-control car, and some schools are also
      DPS-certified to give the road test itself.
    </p>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      What is not a route is driving unlicensed to get the practice. It is the one shortcut that can end with a conviction rather
      than a license.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">What is the "restricted driver license" DPS asks for at a third-party test?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      We do not know, and we will not guess. DPS&rsquo;s third-party skills testing page lists &ldquo;a valid restricted driver
      license&rdquo; among the requirements for applicants 18 and over. What that means for someone holding no Texas credential at all
      is not spelled out on the page. If you are planning to use a third-party road test, ask the school or DPS how it applies to you
      before you pay anything.
    </p>
  </section>
</AdeGuide>
